          "passage": "For decades, public-health researchers studying cholera outbreaks in coastal Bangladesh treated each new wave as the product of local, short-term factors: contaminated water in a particular district, the failure of a particular sanitation system, or the displacement of people by a particular flood. The theoretical ecologist Mercedes Pascual, working with collaborators at the University of Chicago, has used long records of monthly case counts to argue that this district-by-district frame is incomplete. Her statistical models show that the year-to-year variation in cholera incidence across the country tracks the El Niño–Southern Oscillation, a Pacific climate signal that alters sea-surface temperatures, monsoon timing, and the salinity of coastal waters where the cholera bacterium persists. The local explanations, on Pascual's account, are not wrong; they describe the immediate triggers of any single outbreak. But the rhythm of when outbreaks come, and how severe they tend to be in a given year, is set by a much larger climate cycle whose footprint is visible only when the case data are analyzed across years rather than within them.",
