          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "By the late 1920s, astrophysicists believed that aging stars exhausted their nuclear fuel and settled into a uniform, dense end state called a white dwarf. Working aboard a ship bound for England in 1930, the Indian-born theorist Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar carried out a quantum-mechanical calculation that complicated this tidy picture. His result, later named the Chandrasekhar limit, showed that the supporting pressure inside a white dwarf comes from the resistance of densely packed electrons to further compression, but that this pressure has a ceiling: above roughly 1.4 times the mass of the Sun, even fully compressed electrons cannot push back hard enough against gravity. A star ending its life with too much mass, in other words, cannot stop at the white-dwarf stage. Although Arthur Eddington publicly disputed the calculation for years, Chandrasekhar's argument was eventually accepted and reframed the field's account of stellar death: not every star quietly contracts into a stable cinder; the heaviest must continue collapsing, leading to outcomes — neutron stars and black holes — that the simpler picture could not allow.",
