          "difficulty": "hard",
          "band": 4,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "The Turkish bioarchaeologist Yilmaz Selim Erdal has examined dental remains from successive layers of the Neolithic settlement at Çatalhöyük, where small mud-brick houses were rebuilt on the same plots over many generations. Across the lowest, earliest layers of the site, Erdal finds heavy chipping and severe enamel wear on the molars of adult skeletons of both sexes, in patterns ethnographic comparisons associate with grinding coarse cereals on stone querns. In the higher, later layers, that pattern persists in the molars of female skeletons but largely disappears from the molars of male skeletons of comparable age. No comparable change appears in the kinds of grain remains found in the houses, or in the architecture of the food-preparation areas; what shifts, in the higher layers, is which adults' teeth bear the wear. On the basis of these patterns, Erdal's data support the inference that, by the later phases of Çatalhöyük, ______",
