          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "When evolutionary biologist Lynn Margulis published \"On the Origin of Mitosing Cells\" in 1967, she revived an older hypothesis that the major energy-producing structures inside complex cells — mitochondria, which respire, and chloroplasts, which photosynthesize — had originated as free-living bacteria that were engulfed by ancestral host cells and then retained as long-term partners. By the time Margulis was writing, the idea had been all but abandoned, and the standard explanation pictured these organelles as having developed gradually from internal membranes of the host cell itself. *Margulis hypothesized, against this picture, that mitochondria and chloroplasts retain features that would only be expected if they had originated as separate, formerly independent bacterial cells now living inside another cell.*",
