          "type": "multiple-choice",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "band": 4,
          "domain": "expression-of-ideas",
          "skill": "transitions",
          "passage": "In the second half of the twentieth century, scholarship on the citizenship of Indigenous peoples in North America largely treated the question as one of incorporation: how, when, and on what terms had Indigenous individuals been added to the political communities of Canada and the United States? In *Mohawk Interruptus: Political Life Across the Borders of Settler States* (2014), the anthropologist Audra Simpson reframes the question entirely. ______ instead of asking how Mohawk people of Kahnawake have been incorporated into settler citizenship, she examines a community that has long refused that citizenship outright, and treats the act of refusal as itself a substantive political stance — one that exposes settler sovereignty as ongoing negotiation rather than settled fact.",
