          "domain": "craft-and-structure",
          "skill": "text-structure-and-purpose",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Thomas Hardy's 1874 novel *Far from the Madding Crowd*. The sheep farmer Gabriel Oak has just lost most of his flock and his livelihood in a single night and is walking out at dawn to consider what to do.\n\nGabriel walked alone over the down. The sky was the colour of unpolished pewter, and the air had in it that thin damp coolness which, in the country, often follows a long mild night. *He had nothing now but the clothes he stood in, the price of a horse he had not yet sold, and a peculiar steady habit of seeing things as they were that he had inherited from his father and had never been able to put down.* The labourers in the cottages he passed were stirring already; from a chimney here and there came the faint blue smoke of a kindled fire, and a girl with a pail crossed a yard, and another, behind a hedge, was singing to herself as she fed fowls. Gabriel looked at these small busy lives the way one looks at small running streams when one has time to think and has been at a great loss: with attention, but without envy, and as if their order had something to teach him.",
