              "text": "The microbiologist Liping Zhao has spent more than two decades arguing that a small set of \"foundation\" bacterial species inside the human gut do not merely correlate with metabolic health but causally help to determine it. His most cited intervention studies recruit patients with type 2 diabetes and place them on diets rich in non-digestible plant fibers; over the weeks that follow, certain short-chain-fatty-acid-producing taxa expand in the gut community, others recede, and several of his trials report improvements in glycemic control that track those microbial shifts more closely than they track changes in body weight or insulin medication. From this convergence — defined microbial responders, dietary inputs his team can specify, and clinical endpoints that move with the microbial response — Zhao concludes that targeted interventions on the microbiome can produce real, mechanistically explicable improvements in metabolic disease, and that the field's reluctance to treat such relationships as causal underestimates what carefully designed studies have already shown."
