          "passage": "In her 1999 book *The Boundaries of Blackness: AIDS and the Breakdown of Black Politics*, the political scientist Cathy J. Cohen examines why mainstream Black political organizations in the United States were slow to take up the AIDS epidemic during its first decade. The standard explanation at the time pointed to broader social stigma against the disease. Cohen argues that this account is incomplete. Drawing on archival material from major civil-rights and church-affiliated organizations, she develops a different framing: within Black communities themselves, the people most directly affected by AIDS in the 1980s — particularly gay men, intravenous drug users, and trans women — were already on the margins of who counted as a political constituent. The slow response, on Cohen's account, reflected not simply external prejudice but what she calls *secondary marginalization*: a pattern by which an already-marginalized group itself contracts the boundaries of who its politics will publicly defend.",
