          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "When the Yankton Sioux scholar Ella Cara Deloria published *Speaking of Indians* in 1944, U.S. federal policy toward Indigenous nations was being framed almost entirely around the bounded nuclear family — a household consisting of two parents and their children — as the social unit through which welfare benefits, tribal-roll calculations, and assimilation programs ought to operate. Deloria, who had spent decades transcribing Dakota and Lakota narratives and collecting kinship terminology in the field, argued that this framing fundamentally misread Sioux social life. Sioux households, on her account, were the visible nodes of a much larger lattice of obligation in which a wide range of relatives — not only parents and children but a person's older sisters, mother's brothers, father's sisters, and many others — carried specific, ongoing duties of care, instruction, and provisioning. To replace this lattice with the bounded nuclear family for administrative purposes, she contended, was not to record Sioux society more clearly but to deform it.",
