          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "When the ophthalmologist Patricia Bath proposed her Laserphaco Probe in the early 1980s, the standard surgical treatment for cataracts already used ultrasonic energy to fragment and remove a clouded lens. The improvement she described in her 1988 patent was therefore not the introduction of energy-assisted cataract surgery as such — that was already routine — but the substitution of laser energy, applied through a probe of her design, for the ultrasonic shearing of the lens. The change, on her account, mattered because the same operation could be performed with greater precision and less collateral injury to surrounding tissue, especially in patients whose lenses had hardened with age. Bath also pursued a second project that she treated as inseparable from the technical one: a community ophthalmology program built on the premise that rates of preventable blindness vary sharply across communities and that addressing them requires combining clinical care with public-health screening rather than relying on either alone.",
