          "id": 638,
          "type": "multiple-choice",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "band": 3,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "In *The Spirit Catches You and You Fall Down* (1997), the writer Anne Fadiman reconstructs a long medical case in northern California involving a young Hmong patient with severe epilepsy and the American physicians who treated her. Fadiman pays close attention to the clinical encounters in which the patient's family was reported as noncompliant with prescribed medication. In her account, the physicians believed they were describing a single shared problem — controlling seizures pharmacologically — and the family understood the same conversations as being about a quite different problem, one rooted in Hmong frameworks of soul, kinship, and ritual obligation. Neither side, Fadiman shows, recognized that the disagreement lay below the level of treatment refusal: each was pursuing a coherent course of action under its own model of what was happening. On the basis of these observations, Fadiman's case study supports the inference that ______",
