          "band": 4,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "When the Mexican-born archaeologist Zelia Nuttall acquired and published a Mixtec pictorial manuscript in 1902, scholars of pre-Columbian Mexico were divided over what kind of text such manuscripts were. One school had argued that the recurrent figures, calendrical glyphs, and named places strung across the codex pages were freely associative religious imagery without consistent reference. Nuttall took a different view. Working systematically across many pages, she traced repeated combinations of named individuals, place glyphs, and dates and showed that those combinations recur with an internal consistency: the same individuals appear with the same place glyphs, in the same chronological positions, across multiple panels. She did not assemble a complete dynastic narrative from this material. What her cross-page consistency demonstrated, she argued, was that the manuscript was best read as recording particular genealogies and political alliances of pre-Columbian noble lineages, rather than as freely associative religious imagery. From these observations, Nuttall's analysis supports the inference that the disagreement among earlier scholars about the nature of such manuscripts primarily reflected ______",
