          "passage": "When the curator and writer Margie West, then at the Museum and Art Gallery of the Northern Territory, published *The Inspired Dream: Life as Art in Aboriginal Australia* in 1988, Western audiences were often introduced to Aboriginal bark paintings from Arnhem Land as visually striking but interpretively self-evident objects — pictures one could read by looking. West rejected this framing as too thin. *She argued that a single bark painting from Arnhem Land typically operates on more than one level at once, with images and motifs that are open to any viewer overlaid by ancestral content whose meaning is reserved for those who have undergone the appropriate stages of community initiation.*",
